      "end_mono": 307535.517560875,
      "raw_response": " ANSWER: SUPPORTED\n\nThe abstract provides evidence that CX3CR1 on Th2 cells promotes T cell survival. It states that CX3CR1-deficient mice or wild-type (WT) mice treated with CX3CR1-blocking reagents show reduced lung disease upon allergen sensitization and challenge, and that transfer of WT CD4(+) T cells into CX3CR1-deficient mice restores the cardinal features of asthma. The study also found that CX3CR1 signaling promoted Th2 survival in the inflamed lungs, suggesting that CX3CR1 is required for airway inflammation by promoting T helper cell survival and maintenance in inflamed lung.",
