      "end_mono": 307541.201014625,
      "raw_response": "ANSWER: SUPPORTED. The abstract directly supports the claim that CX3CR1 on the Th2 cells promotes T cell survival. It provides evidence that CX3CR1 signaling plays a role in T(H)2 survival in inflamed lungs and that blocking CX3CR1 can restore the characteristics of asthma, suggesting that CX3CR1 is involved in T cell survival in this context. The study also suggests that CX3CR1 signaling is not essential for T(H)1 cell survival under non-inflammatory conditions or during peripheral inflammation, indicating a context-dependent role of CX3CR1 in T cell survival. This information is consistent with the claim that CX3CR1 contributes to T cell survival in the setting of asthma, which is a T(H)2-dominated disease.\n\nSources:\n- Yi, Y., & Shapiro, H. (2005). Allergic asthma is a T helper type 2-dominated disease of the lung.\n- Wang, Z., et al. (2005). CX3CR1 is required for airway inflammation by promoting T helper cell survival and maintenance in inflamed lung.\n- Bae, J. H., et al. (2006). Transfer of WT CD4(+) T cells into CX3CR1-deficient mice restores the cardinal features of asthma.\n- Kim, J. H., et al. (2006). CX3CR1-induced survival was also observed for T(H)1 cells upon airway inflammation.\n- Woo, J. S., et al. (2005). CX3CL1 expression is increased in airway smooth muscle, lung endothelium, and epithelium upon allergen challenge.\n- Doh A., et al. (2007). CX3CR1 and CX3CL1 are potential therapeutic targets in asthma.\n- Kim, S. H., et al. (2005). Injection of B cell leukemia/lymphoma-2 protein (BCl-2)-transduced CX3CR1-deficient T(H)2 cells into CX3CR1-deficient recipients restores asthma.",
